Option 1: use script + manual processes
+ Downtime: 15-30s
+ Recovery handling depends on manual processes
+ No need to install additional services

Option 2: add a new sentinel 
+ Downtime: 5s-7s
+ Recovery handling auto
+ Easier handling in failures

Option 3: use Redis cluster
+ Downtime: 0s
+ Remove sentinel
+ Major change in architecture
+ More complex, requires understanding of slots
+ Suitable for large, sharded environments

-----------------------script-----------------------
- Server-01(master+sentinel): 192.168.10.1
- Server-02(slave+sentinel):  192.168.10.2
1) Shutdown server Server-01 => server-02 slave
2) Swith server-02: slave => master
  - redis-cli -p 7001 SLAVEOF NO ONE
3) Update sentinel server-02
  - redis-cli -p 7000 SENTINEL remove mymaster
  - redis-cli -p 7000 SENTINEL monitor mymaster 192.168.10.1 7000 2
4) Up server Server-01 
5) Update sentinel server-01
  - redis-cli -p 7000 SENTINEL remove mymaster
  - redis-cli -p 7000 SENTINEL monitor mymaster 192.168.10.1 7000 2
6) Swith server-01: master => slave
  - redis-cli -p 7001 SLAVEOF 192.168.10.2 7001
